 "nodes": [
  {
   "id": 1,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 20,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "CA3 PATTERN COMPLETION. A recurrent excitatory population with strong internal connectivity and global inhibition, the classic autoassociative memory circuit. A cue to one end of the band recruits the rest of the population through the recurrent collaterals.",
    "width": 340
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": 2,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 190,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "Watch the raster: the cue drives a few hundred cells, and within about a hundred milliseconds the rest of the band joins them, then the whole thing settles back to its spontaneous rate. Measured 2026-08-29: baseline 0.3 Hz, cued cells 31 Hz and recruited cells 32 Hz during the event, mean 0.58 Hz over 15 s. CA3 pyramidal cells fire under 1 Hz median in an awake animal (Mizuseki and Buzsaki 2013) and reach tens of Hz inside a place field, so those are the right numbers.",
    "width": 340
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": 3,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 430,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "This scene used to claim a persistent attractor and did not have one. Measured 2026-08-29 it ran at 193 Hz mean with every cell active and the same rate before and after the quench, which is a saturated network rather than a stored state. Three things were missing: an absolute refractory period, short-term depression, and enough inhibition. Depression is what makes it robust rather than tuned; with it the response moves 14 percent while the recurrent weight moves 64, so the scene is no longer balanced on a knife edge.",
    "width": 340
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
